t at $Q$. Then $t_P \\cap t_Q \\in EF$.\n\nNow, $X = BQ \\cap DT$.\n\nLet me try to use a more algebraic approach.\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Let me place the circle as the unit circle and use complex numbers.\n\nLet $a, b, c$ be the complex numbers for $A, B, C$ on the unit circle.\n\n$D$ is on ray $BA$, so $D$ is on line $AB$ with $D$ on the ray from $B$ through $A$.\n\nIn complex numbers, $D$ is on line $AB$ iff $\\frac{d - a}{b - a} \\in \\mathbb{R}$, i.e., $d = a + t(b - a)$ for real $t$, or equivalently $d = (1-t)a + tb$ for real $t$.\n\nSince $D$ is on ray $BA$ (from $B$ through $A$), we have $D = B + s(A - B)$ for $s > 0$, i.e., $d = b + s(a - b)$ for $s > 0$.\n\nSo $d = (1-s)b + sa$ for $s > 0$.\n\nThe polar of $D$ is the line $\\bar{d}z + d\\bar{z} = 2$.\n\n$E$ and $F$ are the intersections of this line with the unit circle.\n\n$T = EF \\cap AC$.\n\nLet me compute $T$. $T$ is on line $AC$ and on the polar of $D$.\n\nLine $AC$ in complex: a point $z$ is on line $AC$ iff $\\frac{z-a}{c-a} \\in \\mathbb{R}$, i.e., $z = a + u(c - a)$ for real $u$, or equivalently $\\frac{z-a}{c-a} = \\frac{\\bar{z}-\\bar{a}}{\\bar{c}-\\bar{a}}$.\n\nSince $a, c$ are on the unit circle: $\\bar{a} = 1/a$, $\\bar{c} = 1/c$.\n\n$\\frac{z - a}{c - a} = \\frac{\\bar{z} - 1/a}{1/c - 1/a} = \\frac{\\bar{z} - 1/a}{(a-c)/(ac)} = \\frac{ac(\\bar{z} - 1/a)}{a-c} = \\frac{ac\\bar{z} - c}{a-c}$\n\nSo: $\\frac{z-a}{c-a} = \\frac{ac\\bar{z} - c}{a-c} = -\\frac{ac\\bar{z} - c}{c-a}$\n\nTherefore: $z - a = -(ac\\bar{z} - c) = c - ac\\bar{z}$\n$z + ac\\bar{z} = a + c$\n\nSo the condition for $z$ to be on line $AC$ is: $z + ac\\bar{z} = a + c$.\n\nThe polar of $D$: $\\bar{d}z + d\\bar{z} = 2$.\n\n$T$ satisfies both:\n1. $t + ac\\bar{t} = a + c$\n2. $\\bar{d}t + d\\bar{t} = 2$\n\nFrom (1): $\\bar{t} = \\frac{a + c - t}{ac}$\n\nSubstituting into (2):\n$\\bar{d}t + d \\cdot \\frac{a + c - t}{ac} = 2$\n$\\bar{d}t + \\frac{d(a+c)}{ac} - \\frac{d}{ac}t = 2$\n$t\\left(\\bar{d} - \\frac{d}{ac}\\right) 