B; E, F) = -1$ and $(P, Q; E, F) = -1$.\n\nThe cross ratio $(A, P; B, Q)$: Let me use the \"cross ratio formula.\" If I have four points $A, P, B, Q$ on a circle, and I know $(A, B; E, F) = (P, Q; E, F) = -1$, then:\n\n$(A, P; B, Q) = ?$\n\nLet me use the fact that for four points on a circle, the cross ratio is a real number. And I can compute it using the formula:\n\nIf the involution with fixed points $E, F$ maps $A \\to B$ and $P \\to Q$, then the cross ratio $(A, P; B, Q) = (A, P; f(A), f(P))$ where $f$ is the involution.\n\nFor an involution $f$ with fixed points $E, F$, I can write (in a suitable coordinate): if $E = 0$ and $F = \\infty$, then $f(t) = k/t$ for some constant $k$. The condition $(A, B; E, F) = -1$ means $(a, k/a; 0, \\infty) = -1$.\n\n$(a, k/a; 0, \\infty) = \\frac{a \\cdot \\infty}{k/a \\cdot 0}$... this isn't well-defined. Let me use the formula:\n$(z_1, z_2; z_3, z_4) = \\frac{(z_1 - z_3)(z_2 - z_4)}{(z_1 - z_4)(z_2 - z_3)}$\n\nWith $z_3 = 0, z_4 = \\infty$: $(z_1, z_2; 0, \\infty) = \\frac{z_1 \\cdot z_2}{z_1 \\cdot z_2} = 1$? That's not right.\n\nActually, $(z_1, z_2; 0, \\infty) = \\frac{(z_1 - 0)/(z_1 - \\infty)}{(z_2 - 0)/(z_2 - \\infty)} = \\frac{z_1/(-1)}{z_2/(-1)} = \\frac{z_1}{z_2}$... hmm, let me be more careful.\n\n$(z_1, z_2; z_3, z_4) = \\frac{z_1 - z_3}{z_2 - z_3} \\cdot \\frac{z_2 - z_4}{z_1 - z_4}$\n\nWith $z_3 = 0, z_4 = \\infty$:\n$= \\frac{z_1}{z_2} \\cdot \\frac{z_2 - \\infty}{z_1 - \\infty} = \\frac{z_1}{z_2} \\cdot \\frac{z_2}{z_1} = 1$\n\nThat gives 1, not -1. So I'm making an error.\n\nLet me use $z_3 = \\infty, z_4 = 0$ instead:\n$(z_1, z_2; \\infty, 0) = \\frac{z_1 - \\infty}{z_2 - \\infty} \\cdot \\frac{z_2 - 0}{z_1 - 0} = \\frac{z_1}{z_2} \\cdot \\frac{z_2}{z_1} = 1$\n\nStill 1. Hmm, that means for any $z_1, z_2$, $(z_1, z_2; \\infty, 0) = 1$. That can't be right.\n\nOh wait, I think I'm confusing the definition. Let me use:\n$(z_1, z_2; z_3, z_4) = \\frac{(z_1 - z_3)(z_2 - z_4)}{(z_2 - z_3)(z_1 - z_4)}$\n\nWith $